{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RRgheVwzkE9xKC2qypoVoM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Widgets\n2\t\n3\tThis folder contains every widget the lesson stream can render. The MVP ships\n4\tsix built-in types (`theory`, `quiz`, `code`, `demo`, `sandbox`, `histogram`)\n5\tplus a `custom` placeholder. Each widget is self-contained: a Zod data schema,\n6\ta React component, an editor form, and a registry entry — no other module\n7\tneeds to know it exists.\n8\t\n9\tThis document explains the contract and walks through the five steps to add\n10\tyour own widget type. The `Histogram/` folder is the working reference\n11\timplementation — copy it, rename, and adjust.\n12\t\n13\t## File layout\n14\t\n15\t```\n16\tsrc/widgets/\n17\t  registry.ts             — central registry mapping type → component/icon/label/accent\n18\t  Widget.tsx              — chrome (header, status badge, accent rail) used by every widget\n19\t  schemas/\n20\t    build.ts              — npm run build:schemas writes <type>.json next to this file\n21\t  <Name>/\n22\t    schema.ts             — Zod schema + inferred TS type\n23\t    <Name>Widget.tsx      — read/render component, props: { data: <Type>Data }\n24\t    <Name>Editor.tsx      — side-panel form, props: { initial, onCancel, onSave }\n25\t    sample.ts             — typed sample data (used by storybook-style fixtures, init skills)\n26\t```\n27\t\n28\t## Steps to add a new widget type\n29\t\n30\tThe five steps below are mandatory in this order — skipping one will produce\n31\ta runtime error or break the schema build.\n32\t\n33\t### 1. Add a CSS var `--widget-<name>` in `src/styles/tokens.css`\n34\t\n35\tThe widget header chrome paints a 1-px accent rail, an icon background tint,\n36\tand the \"in progress\" badge from this single CSS variable. Add a value in\n37\tboth the light and dark theme blocks. Pick a tasteful muted colour distinct\n38\tfrom existing widget accents — they are intentionally low-saturation so the\n39\tcontent stays the focus, not the chrome.\n40\t\n41\t```css\n42\t:root,\n43\t:root[data-theme=\"light\"] {\n44\t  /* ... */\n45\t  --widget-histogram: #4e7a85;   /* muted teal */\n46\t}\n47\t\n48\t:root[data-theme=\"dark\"] {\n49\t  /* ... */\n50\t  --widget-histogram: #82b3bd;   /* lighter for dark backgrounds */\n51\t}\n52\t```\n53\t\n54\tExisting accents (light theme) — avoid clashes:\n55\t\n56\t| widget    | accent     |\n57\t| --------- | ---------- |\n58\t| theory    | `#56524a`  |\n59\t| demo      | `#2563eb`  |\n60\t| quiz      | `#6b3eaa`  |\n61\t| code      | `#0d7a5f`  |\n62\t| sandbox   | `#b45309`  |\n63\t| histogram | `#4e7a85`  |\n64\t\n65\t### 2. Define the Zod data schema in `src/widgets/<Name>/schema.ts`\n66\t\n67\tSchemas are the single source of truth: they drive runtime validation in\n68\t`/api/courses/.../lessons` route handlers, the editor form, and the JSON\n69\tSchema files emitted by `npm run build:schemas`.\n70\t\n71\tKeep schemas minimal and concrete. Use `.refine()` for cross-field invariants\n72\t(e.g. `binEdges.length === counts.length + 1`). Export both the schema and\n73\tthe inferred type so editor and widget can share the type without circular\n74\timports.\n75\t\n76\t```ts\n77\t// src/widgets/Histogram/schema.ts\n78\timport { z } from 'zod';\n79\t\n80\texport const HistogramDataSchema = z\n81\t  .object({\n82\t    binEdges: z.array(z.number()).min(2),\n83\t    counts: z.array(z.number().nonnegative()).min(1),\n84\t  })\n85\t  .refine((d) => d.binEdges.length === d.counts.length + 1, {\n86\t    message: 'binEdges.length must equal counts.length + 1',\n87\t    path: ['binEdges'],\n88\t  });\n89\t\n90\texport type HistogramData = z.infer<typeof HistogramDataSchema>;\n91\t```\n92\t\n93\tThen add the schema to `src/widgets/schemas/build.ts` so `npm run\n94\tbuild:schemas` emits a JSON Schema file alongside the others:\n95\t\n96\t```ts\n97\timport { HistogramDataSchema } from '@/widgets/Histogram/schema';\n98\t\n99\tconst widgets: Record<string, ZodSchema> = {\n100\t  // ... existing entries\n101\t  histogram: HistogramDataSchema,\n102\t};\n103\t```\n104\t\n105\tAfter running `npm run build:schemas` a new `src/widgets/schemas/<name>.json`\n106\tfile is committed alongside the others. Don't hand-edit those files — they\n107\tare regenerated.\n108\t\n109\t### 3. Add an entry to `widgetRegistry`\n110\t\n111\t`src/widgets/registry.ts` is what `Widget.tsx` reads to render the chrome\n112\t(icon, label, accent rail) and what `SectionRenderer` falls through to for\n113\tunknown section types.\n114\t\n115\t```ts\n116\t// src/widgets/registry.ts\n117\timport { BarChart3 } from 'lucide-react';\n118\timport { HistogramWidget } from './Histogram/HistogramWidget';\n119\t\n120\texport type WidgetType =\n121\t  | 'theory' | 'quiz' | 'code' | 'demo' | 'sandbox'\n122\t  | 'histogram'   // ← add to the union\n123\t  | 'custom';\n124\t\n125\texport const widgetRegistry: Record<WidgetType, WidgetRegistryEntry> = {\n126\t  // ... existing entries\n127\t  histogram: {\n128\t    component: HistogramWidget as ComponentType<{ data?: unknown }>,\n129\t    label: 'Histogram',\n130\t    icon: BarChart3,                    // any lucide-react icon\n131\t    accentVar: '--widget-histogram',    // matches step 1\n132\t  },\n133\t};\n134\t```\n135\t\n136\tEach entry needs all four fields:\n137\t\n138\t- **`component`** — the read-mode React component. Receives `{ data }` typed\n139\t  as `unknown` (the registry erases the per-widget type so the registry can\n140\t  stay generic). Cast to the concrete type inside the component, or wrap in\n141\t  a typed adapter — `HistogramWidget` accepts `HistogramData` directly.\n142\t- **`label`** — the short human label shown in the widget header eyebrow\n143\t  (\"HISTOGRAM · §3\"). Keep it ≤ 16 chars.\n144\t- **`icon`** — a lucide-react icon component (default size 14, no need to\n145\t  pass it). See <https://lucide.dev/icons/> for options. Pick a glyph that\n146\t  makes the widget recognisable when scanning the lesson stream.\n147\t- **`accentVar`** — the CSS variable name (with leading `--`) that the chrome\n148\t  uses for the rail and icon tint. Must match step 1 exactly.\n149\t\n150\t### 4. Add a per-widget editor form\n151\t\n152\tThe editor opens in a 320-px side panel when the user clicks the pencil\n153\tbutton on a section header (US-023 pattern). Each editor is a small\n154\tcontrolled form that:\n155\t\n156\t1. Hydrates from `initial: <Type>Data`.\n157\t2. Tracks dirty state vs. `initial`.\n158\t3. Calls `<Type>DataSchema.safeParse` before saving and surfaces issues\n159\t   inline via `EditorFormSaveError` / per-field `errorTextStyle`.\n160\t4. Calls `onSave(next)` with the parsed data; the parent persists via\n161\t   `PUT /api/courses/<slug>/lessons/<lessonSlug>`.\n162\t5. Calls `onCancel()` to close without saving.\n163\t\n164\tUse the helpers in `@/components/EditorForm` so every editor looks the same:\n165\t\n166\t```tsx\n167\t// src/widgets/Histogram/HistogramEditor.tsx (excerpt)\n168\timport {\n169\t  EditorFormFooter, EditorFormSaveError,\n170\t  fieldStyle, formBodyStyle, inputStyle, labelStyle,\n171\t  errorTextStyle,\n172\t} from '@/components/EditorForm';\n173\t\n174\texport function HistogramEditor({ initial, onCancel, onSave }) {\n175\t  // ... state, parse, dirty checks\n176\t  return (\n177\t    <div style={{ display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'column', flex: 1, minHeight: 0 }}>\n178\t      <div style={formBodyStyle}>\n179\t        <label style={fieldStyle}>\n180\t          <span style={labelStyle}>Bin edges (comma-separated)</span>\n181\t          <input style={inputStyle} ... />\n182\t        </label>\n183\t        {/* ...more fields... */}\n184\t      </div>\n185\t      <EditorFormSaveError message={saveError} />\n186\t      <EditorFormFooter\n187\t        saving={saving}\n188\t        saveDisabled={!dirty}\n189\t        onCancel={onCancel}\n190\t        onSave={() => void handleSave()}\n191\t      />\n192\t    </div>\n193\t  );\n194\t}\n195\t```\n196\t\n197\tThen wire it into the lesson page panel and the section renderer at\n198\t`src/app/courses/[slug]/lessons/[lessonSlug]/page.tsx`:\n199\t\n200\t- Import the editor at the top of the file.\n201\t- Add a branch inside `WidgetEditPanel` that renders the editor when\n202\t  `section?.type === '<name>'` and forwards `onCancel` / `onSave`.\n203\t- Add a branch inside `SectionRenderer` that wires the read-mode body and a\n204\t  pencil button so the user can open the panel from the widget header.\n205\t\n206\t### 5. (Optional) Extend `SectionSchema`\n207\t\n208\tSteps 1–4 only register the widget for rendering. To allow `<name>` sections\n209\tto live inside lesson JSON files (and thus survive a round-trip through the\n210\tPUT /api/courses/.../lessons handler) you also need to add a section variant\n211\tto the discriminated union in `src/lib/schemas/lesson.ts`:\n212\t\n213\t```ts\n214\timport { HistogramDataSchema } from '@/widgets/Histogram/schema';\n215\t\n216\texport const HistogramSectionSchema = z.object({\n217\t  ...sectionBase,\n218\t  type: z.literal('histogram'),\n219\t  data: HistogramDataSchema,\n220\t});\n221\t\n222\texport const SectionSchema = z.discriminatedUnion('type', [\n223\t  // ... existing variants\n224\t  HistogramSectionSchema,\n225\t]);\n226\t```\n227\t\n228\tWithout this step, the section will still render at runtime (the registry\n229\thas no schema dependency) but lesson JSON containing it will be rejected by\n230\tthe API on save / load. **Always add the union variant unless you have a\n231\tspecific reason not to.**\n232\t\n233\t## Quick checklist\n234\t\n235\tWhen adding a new widget type, all of these must be true before commit:\n236\t\n237\t- [ ] `--widget-<name>` defined in both light and dark theme blocks of `tokens.css`\n238\t- [ ] `src/widgets/<Name>/schema.ts` exports `<Name>DataSchema` + `<Name>Data`\n239\t- [ ] `src/widgets/<Name>/<Name>Widget.tsx` exports `<Name>Widget` taking `{ data }`\n240\t- [ ] `src/widgets/<Name>/<Name>Editor.tsx` exports `<Name>Editor` matching the contract\n241\t- [ ] `src/widgets/<Name>/sample.ts` exports a fixture for fixtures / init skills\n242\t- [ ] `widgetRegistry` has a `<name>` entry (component / label / icon / accentVar)\n243\t- [ ] `WidgetType` union includes `'<name>'`\n244\t- [ ] `src/widgets/schemas/build.ts` includes the schema; `npm run build:schemas` regenerates `<name>.json`\n245\t- [ ] `SectionSchema` discriminated union includes `<Name>SectionSchema` (see step 5)\n246\t- [ ] `WidgetEditPanel` and `SectionRenderer` in the lesson page render the new editor + pencil button\n247\t- [ ] `npm run typecheck` is clean\n248\t\n249\tThe Histogram widget added in US-030 follows every step above and is the\n250\trecommended copy-paste starting point for a new widget.\n251\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"46b898f2-0f25-403b-8a34-d68b7e60e547","uuid":"27c1d848-de3b-4e7e-922d-852fb37843b5","timestamp":"2026-05-03T22:28:47.811Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/nukeemann/projects/ai_lecturer/.worktrees/US-048/src/widgets/README.md","content":"# Widgets\n\nThis folder contains every widget the lesson stream can render. The MVP ships\nsix built-in types (`theory`, `quiz`, `code`, `demo`, `sandbox`, `histogram`)\nplus a `custom` placeholder. Each widget is self-contained: a Zod data schema,\na React component, an editor form, and a registry entry — no other module\nneeds to know it exists.\n\nThis document explains the contract and walks through the five steps to add\nyour own widget type. The `Histogram/` folder is the working reference\nimplementation — copy it, rename, and adjust.\n\n## File layout\n\n```\nsrc/widgets/\n  registry.ts             — central registry mapping type → component/icon/label/accent\n  Widget.tsx              — chrome (header, status badge, accent rail) used by every widget\n  schemas/\n    build.ts              — npm run build:schemas writes <type>.json next to this file\n  <Name>/\n    schema.ts             — Zod schema + inferred TS type\n    <Name>Widget.tsx      — read/render component, props: { data: <Type>Data }\n    <Name>Editor.tsx      — side-panel form, props: { initial, onCancel, onSave }\n    sample.ts             — typed sample data (used by storybook-style fixtures, init skills)\n```\n\n## Steps to add a new widget type\n\nThe five steps below are mandatory in this order — skipping one will produce\na runtime error or break the schema build.\n\n### 1. Add a CSS var `--widget-<name>` in `src/styles/tokens.css`\n\nThe widget header chrome paints a 1-px accent rail, an icon background tint,\nand the \"in progress\" badge from this single CSS variable. Add a value in\nboth the light and dark theme blocks. Pick a tasteful muted colour distinct\nfrom existing widget accents — they are intentionally low-saturation so the\ncontent stays the focus, not the chrome.\n\n```css\n:root,\n:root[data-theme=\"light\"] {\n  /* ... */\n  --widget-histogram: #4e7a85;   /* muted teal */\n}\n\n:root[data-theme=\"dark\"] {\n  /* ... */\n  --widget-histogram: #82b3bd;   /* lighter for dark backgrounds */\n}\n```\n\nExisting accents (light theme) — avoid clashes:\n\n| widget    | accent     |\n| --------- | ---------- |\n| theory    | `#56524a`  |\n| demo      | `#2563eb`  |\n| quiz      | `#6b3eaa`  |\n| code      | `#0d7a5f`  |\n| sandbox   | `#b45309`  |\n| histogram | `#4e7a85`  |\n\n### 2. Define the Zod data schema in `src/widgets/<Name>/schema.ts`\n\nSchemas are the single source of truth: they drive runtime validation in\n`/api/courses/.../lessons` route handlers, the editor form, and the JSON\nSchema files emitted by `npm run build:schemas`.\n\nKeep schemas minimal and concrete. Use `.refine()` for cross-field invariants\n(e.g. `binEdges.length === counts.length + 1`). Export both the schema and\nthe inferred type so editor and widget can share the type without circular\nimports.\n\n```ts\n// src/widgets/Histogram/schema.ts\nimport { z } from 'zod';\n\nexport const HistogramDataSchema = z\n  .object({\n    binEdges: z.array(z.number()).min(2),\n    counts: z.array(z.number().nonnegative()).min(1),\n  })\n  .refine((d) => d.binEdges.length === d.counts.length + 1, {\n    message: 'binEdges.length must equal counts.length + 1',\n    path: ['binEdges'],\n  });\n\nexport type HistogramData = z.infer<typeof HistogramDataSchema>;\n```\n\nThen add the schema to `src/widgets/schemas/build.ts` so `npm run\nbuild:schemas` emits a JSON Schema file alongside the others:\n\n```ts\nimport { HistogramDataSchema } from '@/widgets/Histogram/schema';\n\nconst widgets: Record<string, ZodSchema> = {\n  // ... existing entries\n  histogram: HistogramDataSchema,\n};\n```\n\nAfter running `npm run build:schemas` a new `src/widgets/schemas/<name>.json`\nfile is committed alongside the others. Don't hand-edit those files — they\nare regenerated.\n\n### 3. Add an entry to `widgetRegistry`\n\n`src/widgets/registry.ts` is what `Widget.tsx` reads to render the chrome\n(icon, label, accent rail) and what `SectionRenderer` falls through to for\nunknown section types.\n\n```ts\n// src/widgets/registry.ts\nimport { BarChart3 } from 'lucide-react';\nimport { HistogramWidget } from './Histogram/HistogramWidget';\n\nexport type WidgetType =\n  | 'theory' | 'quiz' | 'code' | 'demo' | 'sandbox'\n  | 'histogram'   // ← add to the union\n  | 'custom';\n\nexport const widgetRegistry: Record<WidgetType, WidgetRegistryEntry> = {\n  // ... existing entries\n  histogram: {\n    component: HistogramWidget as ComponentType<{ data?: unknown }>,\n    label: 'Histogram',\n    icon: BarChart3,                    // any lucide-react icon\n    accentVar: '--widget-histogram',    // matches step 1\n  },\n};\n```\n\nEach entry needs all four fields:\n\n- **`component`** — the read-mode React component. Receives `{ data }` typed\n  as `unknown` (the registry erases the per-widget type so the registry can\n  stay generic). Cast to the concrete type inside the component, or wrap in\n  a typed adapter — `HistogramWidget` accepts `HistogramData` directly.\n- **`label`** — the short human label shown in the widget header eyebrow\n  (\"HISTOGRAM · §3\"). Keep it ≤ 16 chars.\n- **`icon`** — a lucide-react icon component (default size 14, no need to\n  pass it). See <https://lucide.dev/icons/> for options. Pick a glyph that\n  makes the widget recognisable when scanning the lesson stream.\n- **`accentVar`** — the CSS variable name (with leading `--`) that the chrome\n  uses for the rail and icon tint. Must match step 1 exactly.\n\n### 4. Add a per-widget editor form\n\nThe editor opens in a 320-px side panel when the user clicks the pencil\nbutton on a section header (US-023 pattern). Each editor is a small\ncontrolled form that:\n\n1. Hydrates from `initial: <Type>Data`.\n2. Tracks dirty state vs. `initial`.\n3. Calls `<Type>DataSchema.safeParse` before saving and surfaces issues\n   inline via `EditorFormSaveError` / per-field `errorTextStyle`.\n4. Calls `onSave(next)` with the parsed data; the parent persists via\n   `PUT /api/courses/<slug>/lessons/<lessonSlug>`.\n5. Calls `onCancel()` to close without saving.\n\nUse the helpers in `@/components/EditorForm` so every editor looks the same:\n\n```tsx\n// src/widgets/Histogram/HistogramEditor.tsx (excerpt)\nimport {\n  EditorFormFooter, EditorFormSaveError,\n  fieldStyle, formBodyStyle, inputStyle, labelStyle,\n  errorTextStyle,\n} from '@/components/EditorForm';\n\nexport function HistogramEditor({ initial, onCancel, onSave }) {\n  // ... state, parse, dirty checks\n  return (\n    <div style={{ display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'column', flex: 1, minHeight: 0 }}>\n      <div style={formBodyStyle}>\n        <label style={fieldStyle}>\n          <span style={labelStyle}>Bin edges (comma-separated)</span>\n          <input style={inputStyle} ... />\n        </label>\n        {/* ...more fields... */}\n      </div>\n      <EditorFormSaveError message={saveError} />\n      <EditorFormFooter\n        saving={saving}\n        saveDisabled={!dirty}\n        onCancel={onCancel}\n        onSave={() => void handleSave()}\n      />\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\nThen wire it into the lesson page panel and the section renderer at\n`src/app/courses/[slug]/lessons/[lessonSlug]/page.tsx`:\n\n- Import the editor at the top of the file.\n- Add a branch inside `WidgetEditPanel` that renders the editor when\n  `section?.type === '<name>'` and forwards `onCancel` / `onSave`.\n- Add a branch inside `SectionRenderer` that wires the read-mode body and a\n  pencil button so the user can open the panel from the widget header.\n\n### 5. (Optional) Extend `SectionSchema`\n\nSteps 1–4 only register the widget for rendering. To allow `<name>` sections\nto live inside lesson JSON files (and thus survive a round-trip through the\nPUT /api/courses/.../lessons handler) you also need to add a section variant\nto the discriminated union in `src/lib/schemas/lesson.ts`:\n\n```ts\nimport { HistogramDataSchema } from '@/widgets/Histogram/schema';\n\nexport const HistogramSectionSchema = z.object({\n  ...sectionBase,\n  type: z.literal('histogram'),\n  data: HistogramDataSchema,\n});\n\nexport const SectionSchema = z.discriminatedUnion('type', [\n  // ... existing variants\n  HistogramSectionSchema,\n]);\n```\n\nWithout this step, the section will still render at runtime (the registry\nhas no schema dependency) but lesson JSON containing it will be rejected by\nthe API on save / load. **Always add the union variant unless you have a\nspecific reason not to.**\n\n## Quick checklist\n\nWhen adding a new widget type, all of these must be true before commit:\n\n- [ ] `--widget-<name>` defined in both light and dark theme blocks of `tokens.css`\n- [ ] `src/widgets/<Name>/schema.ts` exports `<Name>DataSchema` + `<Name>Data`\n- [ ] `src/widgets/<Name>/<Name>Widget.tsx` exports `<Name>Widget` taking `{ data }`\n- [ ] `src/widgets/<Name>/<Name>Editor.tsx` exports `<Name>Editor` matching the contract\n- [ ] `src/widgets/<Name>/sample.ts` exports a fixture for fixtures / init skills\n- [ ] `widgetRegistry` has a `<name>` entry (component / label / icon / accentVar)\n- [ ] `WidgetType` union includes `'<name>'`\n- [ ] `src/widgets/schemas/build.ts` includes the schema; `npm run build:schemas` regenerates `<name>.json`\n- [ ] `SectionSchema` discriminated union includes `<Name>SectionSchema` (see step 5)\n- [ ] `WidgetEditPanel` and `SectionRenderer` in the lesson page render the new editor + pencil button\n- [ ] `npm run typecheck` is clean\n\nThe Histogram widget added in US-030 follows every step above and is the\nrecommended copy-paste starting point for a new widget.\n","numLines":251,"startLine":1,"totalLines":251}}}
